Z||000000||TheHans||01-20-2001||10:14 PM||||Hey gang,<BR>I can't remember how to figure out displacement. Could ya'll refresh my memory.<P>Also wondering how old everyone is, and what you guys do for a livin (besides spend 40 hours a week on the forum).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000001||StuGood||01-20-2001||10:25 PM||||Assuming that you're measuring everything in inches, and all cylinders have the same bore and stroke,<P>C.I.D = Bore squared, times pi (3.14159), divided by 4, times Stroke, times number of cylinders.     <P>Oh, and I'm a 39-year-old mechanical engineer.  I work for one of the aircraft companies in Wichita. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||01-21-2001||05:32 PM||||Stu correct me if I'm wrong, but I think you have a partial CFM calculation there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> The displacement should (((((Bore * 1/2)squared) * Pi)*Stroke) * No. of Cyl).  A single cylinder disp is: (Pi * Radius squared) * height.  This works for english or metric but remember to keep the units consistent.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||01-21-2001||08:47 PM||||Basic math 87GT ((bore *1/2)** 2) * pi  is the same as  ((bore ** 2)* pi/4||||reg||1
